<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<section xml:id="section-1" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>The Painleve Problem</title>
  
    
  <definition xml:id="def-removable">
    <title>Removable Set</title>
    
    
    <statement>
      <p>
        A compact set <m>E \subseteq \mathbb C</m> is said to be <term>removable for bounded analytic functions</term> if for any open set <m>\Omega</m> containing <m>E</m> every bounded function analytic on <m>\Omega \setminus E</m> has an analytic extension to <m>E</m>.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </definition>

  <p>
    Example: A single point is removable. This is the Riemann Removable Singularities Theorem
  </p>

  <p>
    Non-Example: The unit disk is not removable. Let <m>f(z) = \frac{1}{z}</m>. <m>f</m> is bounded and analytic on <m>\mathbb C \setminus \Delta</m> but has no analytic extension to <m>\mathbb C</m>
  </p>

  <p>
    The Painleve problem is to determine which sets are removable for bounded analytic functions. To solve this problem Ahlfor's introduced the notion of anlaytic capacity.
  </p>

  <definition xml:id="def-admissible">
    <title>Admissible Function</title>
    
    
    <statement>
      <p>
        A function <m>f</m> is said to be <term>admissible</term> for a set <m>E</m> if:
        <ol>
          <li>
            <p>
              <m>f</m> is analytic on <m>\hat{C} \setminus E</m>
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              <m>|f(z)| \leq 1</m> on <m>\hat{C} \setminus E</m> 
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              <m>f(\infty) = 0</m>
            </p>
          </li>
        </ol>
      </p>
    </statement>
  </definition>

  <definition xml:id="def-analytic-cap">
    <title>Analytic Capacity</title>
    
    
    <statement>
      <p>
        The <term>analytic capacity</term> of a compact set <m>E</m> is denoted <m>\gamma(E)</m> and defined by <m>\gamma(E) = \sup |f'(\infty)|</m> where the supremum is taken over all admissible functions on <m>E</m>.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </definition>

  <p>
    Note that <m>f'(\infty) \neq \displaystyle \lim_{z\to \infty} f'(z)</m>. In fact, <m>\displaystyle f'(\infty) = \lim_{z\to\infty} zf(z)</m>
  </p>

  <theorem xml:id="thm-f-prime-infty">
    <statement>
      <p>
        <md>
          f'(\infty) =  \lim_{z\to \infty} z(f(z)-f(\infty))
        </md>
        
      </p>
    </statement>
  </theorem>

  <proof>
    <p>
       Let <m>g(w) = f(\frac{1}{w})</m>. Then we define 
       <md>
        f'(\infty) = g'(0) = \lim_{w\to 0}\frac{g(w)-g(0)}{w-0}.
       </md>
       Let <m>z = \frac{1}{w}</m>. Then
       <md>
        f'(\infty) = \lim_{z\to \infty} \frac{g(\frac{1}{z})-g(0)}{\frac{1}{z}} = \lim_{z\to \infty} z(f(z)-f(\infty)).
       </md>
       
    </p>
  </proof>
  

  <p>
    Another interpretation of <m>f'(\infty)</m> comes from the Laurent series expansion of <m>f</m> at the point at <m>\infty</m>
  </p>

  <p>
    Suppose <m>f</m> is an analytic function in a neighborhood of <m>\infty</m> and let <m>g(z) = f(\frac{1}{z})</m>. Then <m>g</m> is analytic in a neighborhood of <m>0</m> and thus has a Taylor series expansion
    <md>
      g(z) = a_0 + a_1 z + a_2 z^2 \ldots
    </md>
    
  </p>

  <p>
    If <m>f(\infty) = 0</m> then <m>g(0) = 0</m> and hence
    <md>
      g(z) = a_1 z + a_2 z^2 \ldots
    </md>
    
  </p>

  <p>
    Since <m>f(z) = g(\frac{1}{z})</m>, <m>f</m> has a Laurent series expansion at <m>\infty</m> given by
    <md>
      f(z) = \frac{a_1}{z} + \frac{a_2}{z^2} + \ldots
    </md>
    
  </p>

  <p>
    By the formula for Taylor series coefficients
    <md>
      a_1 = g'(0) = \lim_{z\to 0} \frac{g(z)}{z}
    </md>
    
  </p>

  <p>
    Let <m>w = \frac{1}{z}</m>. Then 
    <md>
      g'(0) = \lim_{z\to 0} \frac{g(z)}{z} = \lim_{w\to \infty} w f(w) = f'(\infty)
    </md>
     and thus <m>f'(\infty) = a_1</m>, the coefficient of the <m>\frac{1}{z}</m> term in the Laurent series of <m>f</m> at <m>\infty</m>
  </p>

  <p>
    To show how analytic capacity is used to solve the Painleve problem, Ahlfors restated it as follows
  </p>

  <problem>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Let <m>E</m> be a compact set in <m>\mathbb C</m>. Under what conditions does there exist a non-constant function <m>f(z)</m> which is single-valued, analytic, and bounded outside <m>E</m>
      </p>
    </statement>
  </problem>

  <p>
    Recall that a bounded entire function is constant. This is Liouville's theorem.
  </p>

  <p>
    Ahlfors' restatement of the problem is asking for a characterization of non-removable sets, as opposed to our original statement of the problem which asks to characterize the removable sets.
  </p>

  <theorem xml:id="thm-ahlfors">
    <title>Characterization of Non-removable Sets</title>
    
    
    <statement>
      <p>
        In Ahlfors' statement of the Painleve problem a sufficient and necessary condition for the existence of a function with the required properties is that <m>\gamma(E) &gt; 0</m>
      </p>
    </statement>
  </theorem>

  <proof>
    <p>
      If <m>\gamma(E) &gt; 0</m> then there exists an admissible function <m>f(z)</m> such that <m>f'(\infty) &gt; 0</m>. Said function is analytic and bounded outside <m>E</m> and must be non-constant because if <m>f</m> were constant then <m>f(z) = f(\infty) = 0</m> but 
      <md>
        |f'(\infty)| = \lim_{z\to 0} |zf(z)| &gt; 0.
      </md>
      
    </p>

    <p>
      Conversely suppose there exists a function <m>f</m> with the required properties. By subtracting a constant we may suppose that <m>f(\infty) = 0</m>. Since <m>f</m> is analytic outside <m>E</m> it has a Laurent series expansion at <m>\infty</m> that begins
      <md>
        \frac{c_k}{z^k} + \frac{c_{k+1}}{z^{k+1}} + \ldots
      </md>
       with <m>c_k \neq 0</m>. Multiplying by <m>z^{k-1}</m> gives

       <md>
        \frac{c_k}{z} + \frac{c_{k+1}}{z^2} + \ldots
       </md>
       and by multiplying by a suitable constant so that the function is bounded by <m>1</m> we obtain a function that begins
       <md>
        \frac{a}{z}+ \ldots
       </md>
       with <m>a \neq 0</m>.
    </p>

    <p>
      This function is admissible for <m>E</m> and hence
      <md>
        \gamma(E) \geq |a| &gt; 0.
      </md>
      
    </p>
  </proof>

  <p>
    This theorem shows that a set is removable if and only if it is a set with analytic capacity <m>0</m>. The question is now, which sets are sets with analytic capacity <m>0</m>?
  </p>

  <theorem xml:id="thm-">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Let <m>\Delta</m> denote the closed unit disk centered at the origin in the complex plane; that is, <m>\Delta = \{z \in \mathbb C: |z| \leq 1\}</m>. Then <m>\gamma(\Delta) = 1</m>.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </theorem>

  <proof>
    <p>
      Consider the function <m>f(z) = \frac{1}{z}</m>, which maps the region of <m>\mathbb C</m> outside <m>\Delta</m> into <m>\Delta</m>. We will verify that this is an admissible function.
      <ol>
        <li>
          <p>
            <m>f(z)</m> is a rational function whose only singularity occurs at <m>z = 0</m> which is not part of <m>\hat{\mathbb C} \setminus \Delta</m>. Therefore, <m>f(z)</m> is analytic on <m>\hat{\mathbb C} \setminus \Delta</m>.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            On <m>\hat{\mathbb C} \setminus \Delta</m>, <m>|z| \geq 1</m>. Hence <m>f(z) \leq 1</m> on <m>\hat{\mathbb C} \setminus \Delta</m>.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            <m>f(\infty) = \frac{1}{\infty} = 0</m>.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ol>
    </p>

    <p>
      Thus <m>f(z)</m> is an admissible function and hence <m>f'(\infty) \leq \gamma(\Delta)</m>. Since
      <md>
        f'(\infty) = \lim_{z\to \infty} zf(z) = \lim_{z\to \infty} z\cdot \frac{1}{z} = 1
      </md>
      <m>1 \leq \gamma(\Delta)</m>.
    </p>

    <p>
      To show that this lower bound is also an upper bound, suppose that <m>g(z)</m> is another admissible function. and consider the function <m>h(w) = g(\frac{1}{w})</m>, which is an analytic function on the unit disk <m>\{|w|&lt; 1\}</m> that satisfies <m>g(0)  = 0</m> and <m>|g(\frac{1}{w})| \leq 1</m>.
    </p>

    <p>
      By the Schwarz lemma, <m>|h(w)| \leq |w|</m> on the disk <m>\{|w|&lt; 1\}</m>. Setting <m>z = \frac{1}{w}</m> implies that
      <md>
        |g(z)| \leq |\frac{1}{z}| = |f(z)|
      </md>
      for all <m>z \in \hat{\mathbb C} \setminus \Delta</m>. Hence
      <md>
        |g'(\infty)| = \lim_{z\to \infty} |zg(z)| \leq \lim_{z\to \infty} |zf(z)| = |f'(\infty)|
      </md>
      and thus <m>\gamma(\Delta) = f'(\infty) = 1</m>.
    </p>
  </proof>

  <theorem xml:id="thm-schwarz">
    <title>Schwarz Lemma</title>
    
    
    <statement>
      <p>
        Let <m>f(z)</m> be an analytic function on the unit disk <m>\{|z|&lt; 1\}</m> such that
        <ol>
          <li>
            <p>
              <m>|f(z)| \leq 1</m> for all, <m>z</m> and
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              <m>f(0) = 0</m>.
            </p>
          </li>
        </ol>

        Then  <m>|f(z)| \leq |z|</m> and <m>|f'(0)| \leq 1</m>.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </theorem>

  <proof>
    <p>
      Let <m>g(z) = \frac{f(z)}{z}</m>. This function is analytic on <m>\Delta \setminus \{0\}</m> and <m>\displaystyle \lim_{z\to 0} \frac{f(z)}{z} = f'(0)</m> by L'hopital's rule. Thus if we define
      <md>
        g(z) = \begin{cases}
        \dfrac{f(z)}{z} \amp z \neq 0\\
        f'(0) \amp z=0
    \end{cases}
      </md>
      then <m>g(z)</m> is continuous on <m>\Delta</m> and it follows from the Riemann removable singularities theorem that <m>g</m> is also analytic on <m>\Delta</m>.
    </p>

    <p>
      Now consider the closed disk <m>\overline{D}(0,1-\epsilon)</m> for some small <m>\epsilon &gt; 0</m>. On the boundary of the disk,
      <md>
        |g(z)| \leq \frac{1}{1-\epsilon}. 
      </md>
      
    </p>

    <p>
      Hence by the maximum modulus theorem, <m>|g(z)| \leq \frac{1}{1-\epsilon}</m> on <m>\overline{D}(0,1-\epsilon)</m>. Letting <m>\epsilon \to 0</m> implies that <m>|g(z)| \leq 1</m> on <m>\Delta</m>; that is, <m>|f(z)| \leq |z| </m> on <m>\Delta</m>. Also <m>g(0) = f'(0)</m> so <m>|f'(0)| \leq 1</m>.
    </p>
  </proof>
  
  <p>
    Questions to ponder:
    <ol>
      <li>
        <p>
          What is the analytic capacity of the disk centered at the origin with radius <m>r</m>?
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          What is the analytic capacity of a disk of radius <m>r</m> centered at some other point <m>z_0</m>?
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          Can you prove your answers?
        </p>
      </li>
    </ol>
  </p>
  
  


</section>
